---
import TutorialLayout from "../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import { pageBySlug } from "../../lib/tutorial";

const page = pageBySlug("getting-started")!;

const kbd =
	"rounded border border-neutral-300 bg-neutral-50 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs text-neutral-700 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-300";
const code =
	"rounded bg-neutral-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-neutral-800 dark:bg-neutral-800/70 dark:text-neutral-200";
---

<TutorialLayout
	title={page.title}
	description={page.blurb}
	current={page.slug}
>
	<header class="mb-8">
		<p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">
			Tutorial
		</p>
		<h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">{page.title}</h1>
		<p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
			{page.blurb}
		</p>
	</header>

	<div class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">

 
		<h2 class="pt-2 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
			A sound
		</h2>
		<p>The shortest thing that makes noise is one oscillator:</p>
		<Code code="sin(440)" />
		<p>
			Press <kbd class={kbd}>⌘ ,</kbd> — <kbd class={kbd}>Ctrl ,</kbd> on Windows
			and Linux — or the play button, and the tab you are in is evaluated.
			<kbd class={kbd}>⌘ .</kbd> stops the audio. Playing again replaces what is
			running immediately, so the way to change a sound is to edit it and press
			play.
		</p>

		<h2 class="pt-4 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
			Quieter
		</h2>
		<p>
			That is loud. Multiplying a signal scales its amplitude, so this is the
			same tone at a tenth of the volume:
		</p>
		<Code code="sin(440) * 0.1" />
		<p>
			Signals add, too. Two oscillators an octave apart, named once and used
			twice:
		</p>
		<Code
			code={`let freq = 220
(sin(freq) + sin(freq * 2)) * 0.25`}
		/>

		<h2 class="pt-4 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
			An instrument
		</h2>
		<p>
			A file evaluates on its own like this, which is fine for one sound and no
			use once you want that sound played in a pattern. Wrap it in a{" "}
			<code class={code}>fn</code> and it becomes something you can name and
			call — a function that returns a signal is an instrument.
		</p>
		<Code
			code={`fn synth(freq) {
  saw(freq) + saw(freq * 2) * 0.5
}`}
		/>
		<p>
			On its own that does nothing: it is a definition, and nothing has asked
			for a sound yet. Call it, and there is one.
		</p>
		<Code
			code={`fn synth(freq) {
  saw(freq) + saw(freq * 2) * 0.5
}

synth(220)`}

		/>
		<p>
			Not yet inspiring, but a sound — and it will hold that note until you
			press <kbd class={kbd}>⌘ .</kbd>. Every name you can call is in the{" "}
			<a
				href="/docs/"
				class="text-blue-700 hover:underline dark:text-blue-400">function reference</a
			>.
		</p>
	</div>
</TutorialLayout>
